      {
        "id": "p50-q15-a",
        "type": "part",
        "label": "a",
        "prompt": "If $f(x)=\\dfrac{x^2}{1+x^2}$, show that $f'(x)=\\dfrac{2x}{(1+x^2)^2}$ and $f''(x)=\\dfrac{2-6x^2}{(1+x^2)^3}$.",
        "layout": "list",
        "columns": null,
        "questionDiagrams": [],
        "children": [],
        "answer": {
          "short": "$f'(x)=\\dfrac{2x}{(1+x^2)^2}$ and $f''(x)=\\dfrac{2-6x^2}{(1+x^2)^3}$.",
          "worked": "$$\\begin{align*}f'(x)&=\\frac{2x(1+x^2)-x^2(2x)}{(1+x^2)^2}\\\\f'(x)&=\\frac{2x}{(1+x^2)^2}\\\\f''(x)&=\\frac{2(1+x^2)^2-2x\\cdot2(1+x^2)(2x)}{(1+x^2)^4}\\\\f''(x)&=\\frac{2(1+x^2)-8x^2}{(1+x^2)^3}\\\\f''(x)&=\\frac{2-6x^2}{(1+x^2)^3}\\end{align*}$$",
          "solutionDiagrams": []
        },
        "answerSpaceMm": 37
      },
