      {
        "id": "p50-q15-c",
        "type": "part",
        "label": "c",
        "prompt": "Find the coordinates of any points of inflection.",
        "layout": "list",
        "columns": null,
        "questionDiagrams": [],
        "children": [],
        "answer": {
          "short": "$\\left(-\\dfrac1{\\sqrt3},\\dfrac14\\right)$ and $\\left(\\dfrac1{\\sqrt3},\\dfrac14\\right)$.",
          "worked": "$$\\begin{align*}f''(x)&=0\\\\2-6x^2&=0\\\\x^2&=\\frac13\\\\x&=\\pm\\frac1{\\sqrt3}\\\\f\\left(\\pm\\frac1{\\sqrt3}\\right)&=\\frac{1/3}{1+1/3}\\\\f\\left(\\pm\\frac1{\\sqrt3}\\right)&=\\frac14\\end{align*}$$\nThe denominator of $f''(x)$ is always positive. For $x<-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt3}$, $f''(x)<0$; for $-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt3}<x<\\frac{1}{\\sqrt3}$, $f''(x)>0$; for $x>\\frac{1}{\\sqrt3}$, $f''(x)<0$. Concavity changes at both values, so the points of inflection are $\\left(-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt3},\\frac{1}{4}\\right)$ and $\\left(\\frac{1}{\\sqrt3},\\frac{1}{4}\\right)$.",
          "solutionDiagrams": []
        },
        "answerSpaceMm": 34
      },
      {
        "id": "p50-q15-d",
        "type": "part",
        "label": "d",
        "prompt": "State the equation of the horizontal asymptote.",
        "layout": "list",
        "columns": null,
        "questionDiagrams": [],
        "children": [],
        "answer": {
          "short": "$y=1$.",
          "worked": "$$\\begin{align*}f(x)&=1-\\frac1{1+x^2}\\\\\\lim_{x\\to\\pm\\infty}f(x)&=1\\end{align*}$$\nThe horizontal asymptote is $y=1$.",
          "solutionDiagrams": []
        },
        "answerSpaceMm": 10
      },
      {
        "id": "p50-q15-e",
        "type": "part",
        "label": "e",
        "prompt": "Sketch the graph of the function, indicating all important features.",
        "layout": "list",
        "columns": null,
        "questionDiagrams": [],
        "children": [],
        "answer": {
          "short": "Even curve with minimum and sole intercept $(0,0)$, inflection points $\\left(\\pm\\frac{1}{\\sqrt3},\\frac{1}{4}\\right)$ and horizontal asymptote $y=1$, approached from below.",
          "worked": "The domain is $\\mathbb R$ because $1+x^2>0$. Since $f(-x)=f(x)$, the graph is symmetric about the $y$-axis. It decreases to the minimum $(0,0)$ and then increases. Concavity is down for $|x|>\\frac{1}{\\sqrt3}$ and up for $|x|<\\frac{1}{\\sqrt3}$. Mark both points of inflection and draw the curve approaching $y=1$ from below at both ends.",
          "solutionDiagrams": [
